import{_ as a,o as i,c as t,an as o}from"./chunks/framework.DWcCA-MM.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Why Julia?","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"Design/Philosophy/WhyJulia.md","filePath":"Design/Philosophy/WhyJulia.md","lastUpdated":null}'),s={name:"Design/Philosophy/WhyJulia.md"};function n(r,e,l,h,d,u){return i(),t("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<h1 id="WhyJulia" tabindex="-1">Why Julia? <a class="header-anchor" href="#WhyJulia" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why Julia? {#WhyJulia}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>In the section on why we are developing <code>Mantis</code> (see <a href="/Mantis.jl/previews/PR407/Design/Philosophy/WhyMantis#WhyMantis">here</a>), we briefly mentioned choosing Julia as programming language. Here, we detail this choice.</p><p>When we started the <code>Mantis</code>-project, we were not a priori tied to a specific programming language. Already at the start, the expected users were researcher and students like ourselves. We assumed that most researchers and students, just like us, only had some experience in programming for scientific computing purposes. Most likely in languages like Python or MATLAB. This seemed to favour interpreted languages over compiled ones, to reduce the burden on the developers, the potential contributors, and the user.</p><p>Since the goal was to build an open source project, we also wanted to choose an open-source language. So far, a language like Python would fit all our boxes. However, languages like Python often start to suffer performance penalties when trying somewhat larger simulations. One way around that is writing performance critical code in a lower level language. This, however, leads to a two-language problem, which is something that we wanted to avoid.</p><p>Then we learned about the Julia programming language. It was designed to fill precisely this gap (<a href="/Mantis.jl/previews/PR407/BibliographicInformation/references#Bezanson2017">Bezanson <em>et al.</em>, 2017</a>) (<a href="/Mantis.jl/previews/PR407/BibliographicInformation/references#Bezanson2018">Bezanson <em>et al.</em>, 2018</a>). Next to the promise of performance, Julia also introduced dynamic multiple dispatch. This ensures that Julia code is dynamic, easily extensible, and specialisable. That is, you can easily add new code, without performance penalty, and on the fly.</p><p>Julia&#39;s multiple dispatch, and its subsequent specialsation, allow to write general code that specialises to the given situation. This idea is also present when using differential forms. Combining differential forms with Julia allows us to write finite element simulations using differential forms in various geometries and use cases. It also allowed our code to be open, accessible, and extensible.</p><p>This is why we think that the Julia programming language is a great choice for <code>Mantis</code>.</p>',7)])])}const g=a(s,[["render",n]]);export{p as __pageData,g as default};
